<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anatolia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Anatolian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anatolia, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, is the large peninsula forming most of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. It was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> and was later ruled or contested by the Achaemenid, Seleucid and Byzantine empires. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a> in 1071 Turkish groups settled it under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>, and it became the base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anatolia.jpg" alt="Anatolia" width="320" height="150" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Golden · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_geographic_region_of_Anatolia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Anatolia, also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, is a peninsula in West Asia that makes up the majority of the land area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. It is bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> to the south, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> to the west, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a> to the northwest and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to the north. The name comes from the Greek Anatolē, meaning the East, the direction of the rising sun. The oldest known name for a region within it is the Land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatti">Hatti</a>, later used for the whole territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Anatolia was an early centre of farming. About 9,000 years ago a major migration of Anatolian Neolithic farmers into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> began, and Çatalhöyük, dated to about 7,000 BCE, is considered the most advanced of its Neolithic settlements. Written records begin with clay tablets of about 2000 BCE from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanesh">Kültepe</a>, which belonged to an Assyrian trade colony. The earliest attested peoples were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattians">Hattians</a> in the centre and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurrians">Hurrians</a> in the east, followed by Indo-European speakers of Hittite, Luwian and Lydian. Minted coinage first appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> in the 7th century BCE. In the 6th century BCE the Persian Achaemenid Empire conquered all of Anatolia.</p><p>Greek came to dominate the peninsula's languages in the Hellenistic and Roman periods, and by the 4th century CE western and central Anatolia were overwhelmingly Christian and Greek-speaking. In the ten years after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a> in 1071, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> migrated over large areas, beginning the slow change to a Muslim, Turkish-speaking land. Control was divided between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and the Seljuk Sultanate of Rûm. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> swept through the east and centre in 1255 and stayed until 1335. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-i">Osman I</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orhan">Orhan</a>, absorbed the Anatolian beyliks in the 15th century and completed the conquest in 1517.</p><p>Between 1894 and 1924, millions of Christians and other non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkic-peoples">Turkic peoples</a>, among them about two million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and an estimated 1.5 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, were killed or expelled by Ottoman Turkish authorities from most of the area of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anatolia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A single campaign thus gave Cyrus the Median territories, from the Iranian plateau to eastern <b>Anatolia</b> as far as the Halys river.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He then campaigned into Elam to the east and up the Euphrates into Syria, reaching the Cedar Forest and the Silver Mountains towards the Mediterranean, while later legend carried his armies into <b>Anatolia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In November 1467 he surprised and killed the Qara Qoyunlu ruler Jahan Shah in eastern <b>Anatolia</b>, and in 1469 he trapped the Timurid sultan Abu Sa’id, who had invaded Azerbaijan, by cutting his supplies during the winter in the Qarabagh plain; Abu Sa’id was captured and put to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It rested on a network of Assyrian merchant colonies in <b>Anatolia</b>, of which the karum at Kanesh (modern Kültepe in Turkey) was the most important.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It rested on the stability of the gold solidus (later called the nomisma), on Constantinople's position at the crossroads of intercontinental trade, and on the agricultural surpluses of <b>Anatolia</b> and the Balkans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade networks reached from the Persian Gulf to the Levantine coast, and Babylon was an entrepôt for goods moving between Egypt, Arabia, <b>Anatolia</b> and the Iranian plateau.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
